data.text:
The shell function failed because the file list contains newlines and `for f in $files` doesn't split on newlines properly in zsh (zsh doesn't word-split unquoted variables by default). Let me use a different approach — pipe file lists to xargs with a single awk pass, or use wc -l directly on the file lists.

Better approach: use `wc -l` on the whole file list at once for totals, and use awk per-file for blank/comment counting. Actually simpler: use xargs grep -c ... no, let me write a cleaner awk-based one-pass counter that processes each file: for each file, count total lines, blank lines, comment-ish lines.

awk script:
```awk
FNR==1 { files++ }
{ total++ }
/^[[:space:]]*$/ { blank++ }
/^[[:space:]]*(\/\/|\/\*|\*|\/\/\/|<!--|#)/ { comment++ }
END { print total, blank, comment, files }
```

Pass files via xargs. This works for Java/TS/CSS/sh/YAML/XML/HTML with the rough comment regex. For XML/HTML I don't have `#` comments; the `#` pattern would false-positive on JSON but JSON has no comments — minor approximation, acceptable. Actually for config I'll keep it simple.

Note: `*` prefix lines in Java/TS inside block comments — my regex `^[[:space:]]*\*` catches them. Lines like `*/` caught too.

One caveat: awk with 45 files is fine. Use `xargs awk '...'`. Some grep here is ugrep-aliased (interesting, "ugrep: warning"), which behaves differently. Use awk to avoid that.

Let me write:

```bash
cd /Users/honlnk/project/short-drama-web
stats() {
  find ... | xargs awk '
    FNR==1 { files++ }
    { total++ }
    /^[[:space:]]*$/ { blank++; next }
    /^[[:space:]]*(\/\/|\/\*|\*|<!--|#)/ { comment++ }
    END { printf "文件:%d 总行:%d 空行:%d 注释:%d 有效:%d\n", files, total, blank, comment, total-blank-comment }
  '
}
```

For shell scripts, `#` includes shebang — fine.

Note zsh: pipe to xargs handles it. Also careful with apps/web/src vs admin. Let me run.

xargs can't run a shell function. I need to export the function or inline the awk in each xargs. Simplest: define the awk program once in a variable and inline it, or write the whole thing as one awk invocation per find with the awk program inline.

Let me just repeat the awk inline. Or use `sh -c` wrapper. Cleanest: assign AWKPROG and use eval... In zsh, `xargs -0 awk "$AWKPROG"` won't work since awk program is one arg containing spaces — xargs appends filenames as separate args, awk takes the program as first arg. Actually `xargs -0 awk 'program'` works fine — awk 'program' file1 file2... So just inline the awk program in each xargs call.

Let me redo with a loop over labeled groups to avoid repetition:

```bash
cd /Users/honlnk/project/short-drama-web
AWK='
  FNR==1 { files++ }
  { total++ }
  /^[[:space:]]*$/ { blank++; next }
  /^[[:space:]]*(\/\/|\/\*|\*|<!--|#)/ { comment++ }
  END { printf "文件:%d  总行:%d  空行:%d  注释:%d  有效代码:%d\n", files, total, blank, comment, total-blank-comment }
'
run() { echo "$1"; shift; xargs -0 awk "$AWK"; }
```

Hmm, function + xargs issue again — but here xargs runs `awk` (a real binary), not the function. That works! The issue before was `xargs stats` trying to execute function `stats` as a command. Now xargs runs awk directly. Good.

